20:51:21 [5255] [StoryGenerator] prompt:  Write a short story for a 5-year-old female named sophie, who has speech delay. The story will be used by a robot to practise WH-question comprehension (WHO / WHAT / WHERE) with the child.
20:51:21 [5255] 
20:51:21 [5255] --- STYLE REFERENCE (from the curated 4-to-7 corpus, WHO/WHAT/WHERE subset for this age) ---
20:51:21 [5255] Follow the general style, length, vocabulary, and structure of these examples. Each story is followed by 5–7 WH-questions whose answers are explicitly stated in the story.
20:51:21 [5255] Example 1 (setting: bathroom):
20:51:21 [5255]   Story: Kelly is in the bathroom. She is brushing her teeth. Oh no! Kelly drops the toothpaste on the floor. Now there is toothpaste on the floor. Kelly needs to get a cloth to wipe up the mess.
20:51:21 [5255]   Questions:
20:51:21 [5255]   - WHERE is Kelly?
20:51:21 [5255]   - WHAT is she doing?
20:51:21 [5255]   - WHO drops the toothpaste?
20:51:21 [5255]   - WHERE does the toothpaste fall?
20:51:21 [5255]   - WHO needs to clean up the mess?
20:51:21 [5255]   - WHAT will she clean with?
20:51:21 [5255] 
20:51:21 [5255] Example 2 (setting: schoolyard):
20:51:21 [5255]   Story: The children are in the schoolyard. They are playing. The boys are throwing a football. The girls are jumping rope. Oh no, rain clouds are coming! Soon the children will have to go inside.
20:51:21 [5255]   Questions:
20:51:21 [5255]   - WHERE are the children?
20:51:21 [5255]   - WHAT are they doing?
20:51:21 [5255]   - WHO is playing football?
20:51:21 [5255]   - WHAT are the girls doing?
20:51:21 [5255]   - WHAT is coming?
20:51:21 [5255]   - WHERE will the children go?
20:51:21 [5255] 
20:51:21 [5255] --- END STYLE REFERENCE ---
20:51:21 [5255] 
20:51:21 [5255] --- AGE-APPROPRIATE LANGUAGE REQUIREMENTS ---
20:51:21 [5255] Language level: Write concrete sentences in the present tense. Use simple vocabulary a 4–5 year old already knows (ball, beach, dog, bus, kite, snow, library). Each sentence states a single observable fact (who is there, where, what is happening, what surprises them). Avoid abstract concepts, idioms, figurative language, and complex compound sentences. Story structure: One small scene — everyday or imaginative — with a tiny twist or surprise. Examples of scenes: building a sandcastle and finding a crab; flying a kite and the wind blowing it onto a dog; dropping toothpaste on the bathroom floor; spotting an unusual lunch in the cafeteria. Characters: 1–3 named characters; refer to them by name (not pronouns) so questions are answerable. Follow-up questions: After the story, generate 5–7 WH-questions (WHO/WHAT/WHERE ONLY — no HOW/WHY at this age) whose answers are explicitly stated in the story text — one fact per question, in roughly story order.
20:51:21 [5255] 
20:51:21 [5255] --- STORY SETTING AND THEME ---
20:51:21 [5255] Set the story in a familiar, child-friendly environment.
20:51:21 [5255] The obstacle should involve a manageable challenge that requires help from others.
20:51:21 [5255] The resolution should leave the protagonist feeling proud, grateful, and connected.
20:51:21 [5255] 
20:51:21 [5255] Therapy goals to integrate naturally into the story (do NOT list them explicitly — weave them into narrative, dialogue, and action):
20:51:21 [5255] 
20:51:21 [5255] - Learning descriptive words (adjectives, spatial terms)
20:51:21 [5255] - Collaboration and turn-taking
20:51:21 [5255] - Importance of friendships and relationships
20:51:21 [5255] - Overcoming challenges with support
20:51:21 [5255] 
20:51:21 [5255] --- LANGUAGE & INTEREST GUIDANCE (knowledge base) ---
20:51:21 [5255] Target developmental level: age 5, approx MLU 6-8 words per utterance. Keep sentences at or near this length.
20:51:21 [5255] 
20:51:21 [5255] Weave these language targets naturally into narration and dialogue (model them in context; do not drill or quiz them in the story):
20:51:21 [5255] - plural forms (Regular plural nouns): e.g. dogs, cars, apples, cats, toys
20:51:21 [5255] - ing form (Present participle/progressive verb form): e.g. running, jumping, eating, playing
20:51:21 [5255] - pronouns later (Later developing pronouns): e.g. he, she, they, her, his, their
20:51:21 [5255] - location noun (Location concept expressed with a noun phrase): e.g. in the box, on the table, under the chair
20:51:21 [5255] - simple conjunction (Early conjunction use): e.g. and, or
20:51:21 [5255] 
20:51:21 [5255] Practise these target speech sounds by naturally featuring words that contain them (do not turn the story into a pronunciation drill):
20:51:21 [5255] - advanced sounds [th, r, ch] (Late-developing speech sounds for articulation practice.): e.g. thumb, three, rabbit, red, chair, cheese
20:51:21 [5255] 
20:51:21 [5255] Use these interest themes as story hooks, characters, and settings:
20:51:21 [5255] - fairies (fairy, magic wand, wings); princesses (princess, castle, crown); pets (dog, cat, hamster, fish); superheroes (superhero, cape, rescue mission)
20:51:21 [5255] 
20:51:21 [5255] --- TONE AND STYLE ---
20:51:21 [5255] - Warm, simple, and concrete. Describe one small scene — everyday or imaginative — with a tiny surprise or twist.
20:51:21 [5255] - sophie should appear by name in the story (not just "she" / "he"), so WHO-questions are answerable.
20:51:21 [5255] - Use observable facts only (who is there, where they are, what they are doing, what they see/find) — even imaginative characters must do concrete, observable things so the questions are answerable.
20:51:21 [5255] - Do NOT invent moral lessons, internal monologue, or three-act structure. Keep it to the corpus style.
20:51:21 [5255] 
20:51:21 [5255] 
20:51:21 [5255] Return your answer EXACTLY in this format, with no other text before or after:
20:51:21 [5255] 
20:51:21 [5255] ** Title **
20:51:21 [5255] <one short title>
20:51:21 [5255] 
20:51:21 [5255] <the full story text>
20:51:21 [5255] 
20:51:21 [5255] ** End **
20:51:21 [5255] ** Questions **
20:51:21 [5255] 1. <WH-question>
20:51:21 [5255] 2. <WH-question>
20:51:21 [5255] 3. <WH-question>
20:51:21 [5255] 4. <WH-question>
20:51:21 [5255] 5. <WH-question>
20:51:21 [5255] (6. and 7. optional)
20:51:21 [5255] 
20:51:21 [5255] ** Explanation of the output **
20:51:21 [5255] <1–2 short sentences explaining how the story and questions match the selected topic(s) and the learning goals: general speech-language therapy goals>
20:51:21 [5255] 
20:51:21 [5255] STRICT RULES:
20:51:21 [5255] - Do NOT include any preamble, commentary, or meta-text (no "Here is your story", "Sure!", etc.).
20:51:21 [5255] - The story text (between Title and End) MUST be between 70 and 100 words.
20:51:21 [5255] - Generate 5–7 WH-questions using ONLY WHO / WHAT / WHERE (no HOW / WHY at this age tier).
20:51:21 [5255] - Every question's answer MUST appear word-for-word in the story text. Do NOT ask about anything not stated.
20:51:21 [5255] - Refer to characters by name (not pronouns) in the story so questions like "WHO is in the story?" are answerable.
20:51:21 [5255] - Do NOT add sections beyond Title, story text, End, Questions, and Explanation.
