21:05:06 [4a44] [StoryGenerator] prompt:  Write a short story for a 5-year-old female named sophie, who has speech delay. The story will be used by a robot to practise WH-question comprehension (WHO / WHAT / WHERE) with the child.
21:05:06 [4a44] 
21:05:06 [4a44] --- STYLE REFERENCE (from the curated 4-to-7 corpus, WHO/WHAT/WHERE subset for this age) ---
21:05:06 [4a44] Follow the general style, length, vocabulary, and structure of these examples. Each story is followed by 5–7 WH-questions whose answers are explicitly stated in the story.
21:05:06 [4a44] Example 1 (setting: schoolyard):
21:05:06 [4a44]   Story: The children are in the schoolyard. They are playing. The boys are throwing a football. The girls are jumping rope. Oh no, rain clouds are coming! Soon the children will have to go inside.
21:05:06 [4a44]   Questions:
21:05:06 [4a44]   - WHERE are the children?
21:05:06 [4a44]   - WHAT are they doing?
21:05:06 [4a44]   - WHO is playing football?
21:05:06 [4a44]   - WHAT are the girls doing?
21:05:06 [4a44]   - WHAT is coming?
21:05:06 [4a44]   - WHERE will the children go?
21:05:06 [4a44] 
21:05:06 [4a44] Example 2 (setting: autumn / leaves):
21:05:06 [4a44]   Story: The children are playing in the leaves. They are jumping in a big leaf pile. The girl in the red coat feels something under the leaves. She pulls it out. It is a pumpkin! The pumpkin was hidden in the leaf pile.
21:05:06 [4a44]   Questions:
21:05:06 [4a44]   - WHERE are the children?
21:05:06 [4a44]   - WHAT are they doing in the leaves?
21:05:06 [4a44]   - WHO feels something under the leaves?
21:05:06 [4a44]   - WHAT does she do?
21:05:06 [4a44]   - WHAT does she find?
21:05:06 [4a44]   - WHERE was the pumpkin?
21:05:06 [4a44] 
21:05:06 [4a44] --- END STYLE REFERENCE ---
21:05:06 [4a44] 
21:05:06 [4a44] --- AGE-APPROPRIATE LANGUAGE REQUIREMENTS ---
21:05:06 [4a44] Language level: Write concrete sentences in the present tense. Use simple vocabulary a 4–5 year old already knows (ball, beach, dog, bus, kite, snow, library). Each sentence states a single observable fact (who is there, where, what is happening, what surprises them). Avoid abstract concepts, idioms, figurative language, and complex compound sentences. Story structure: One small scene — everyday or imaginative — with a tiny twist or surprise. Examples of scenes: building a sandcastle and finding a crab; flying a kite and the wind blowing it onto a dog; dropping toothpaste on the bathroom floor; spotting an unusual lunch in the cafeteria. Characters: 1–3 named characters; refer to them by name (not pronouns) so questions are answerable. Follow-up questions: After the story, generate 5–7 WH-questions (WHO/WHAT/WHERE ONLY — no HOW/WHY at this age) whose answers are explicitly stated in the story text — one fact per question, in roughly story order.
21:05:06 [4a44] 
21:05:06 [4a44] --- STORY SETTING AND THEME ---
21:05:06 [4a44] Set the story in a familiar, child-friendly environment.
21:05:06 [4a44] The obstacle should involve a manageable challenge that requires help from others.
21:05:06 [4a44] The resolution should leave the protagonist feeling proud, grateful, and connected.
21:05:06 [4a44] 
21:05:06 [4a44] Therapy goals to integrate naturally into the story (do NOT list them explicitly — weave them into narrative, dialogue, and action):
21:05:06 [4a44] 
21:05:06 [4a44] - Learning descriptive words (adjectives, spatial terms)
21:05:06 [4a44] - Collaboration and turn-taking
21:05:06 [4a44] - Importance of friendships and relationships
21:05:06 [4a44] - Overcoming challenges with support
21:05:06 [4a44] 
21:05:06 [4a44] --- LANGUAGE & INTEREST GUIDANCE (knowledge base) ---
21:05:06 [4a44] Target developmental level: age 5, approx MLU 6-8 words per utterance. Keep sentences at or near this length.
21:05:06 [4a44] 
21:05:06 [4a44] Weave these language targets naturally into narration and dialogue (model them in context; do not drill or quiz them in the story):
21:05:06 [4a44] - plural forms (Regular plural nouns): e.g. dogs, cars, apples, cats, toys
21:05:06 [4a44] - ing form (Present participle/progressive verb form): e.g. running, jumping, eating, playing
21:05:06 [4a44] - pronouns later (Later developing pronouns): e.g. he, she, they, her, his, their
21:05:06 [4a44] - location noun (Location concept expressed with a noun phrase): e.g. in the box, on the table, under the chair
21:05:06 [4a44] - simple conjunction (Early conjunction use): e.g. and, or
21:05:06 [4a44] 
21:05:06 [4a44] Practise these target speech sounds by naturally featuring words that contain them (do not turn the story into a pronunciation drill):
21:05:06 [4a44] - advanced sounds [th, r, ch] (Late-developing speech sounds for articulation practice.): e.g. thumb, three, rabbit, red, chair, cheese
21:05:06 [4a44] 
21:05:06 [4a44] Use these interest themes as story hooks, characters, and settings:
21:05:06 [4a44] - fairies (fairy, magic wand, wings); princesses (princess, castle, crown); pets (dog, cat, hamster, fish); superheroes (superhero, cape, rescue mission)
21:05:06 [4a44] 
21:05:06 [4a44] --- TONE AND STYLE ---
21:05:06 [4a44] - Warm, simple, and concrete. Describe one small scene — everyday or imaginative — with a tiny surprise or twist.
21:05:06 [4a44] - sophie should appear by name in the story (not just "she" / "he"), so WHO-questions are answerable.
21:05:06 [4a44] - Use observable facts only (who is there, where they are, what they are doing, what they see/find) — even imaginative characters must do concrete, observable things so the questions are answerable.
21:05:06 [4a44] - Do NOT invent moral lessons, internal monologue, or three-act structure. Keep it to the corpus style.
21:05:06 [4a44] 
21:05:06 [4a44] 
21:05:06 [4a44] Return your answer EXACTLY in this format, with no other text before or after:
21:05:06 [4a44] 
21:05:06 [4a44] ** Title **
21:05:06 [4a44] <one short title>
21:05:06 [4a44] 
21:05:06 [4a44] <the full story text>
21:05:06 [4a44] 
21:05:06 [4a44] ** End **
21:05:06 [4a44] ** Questions **
21:05:06 [4a44] 1. <WH-question>
21:05:06 [4a44] 2. <WH-question>
21:05:06 [4a44] 3. <WH-question>
21:05:06 [4a44] 4. <WH-question>
21:05:06 [4a44] 5. <WH-question>
21:05:06 [4a44] (6. and 7. optional)
21:05:06 [4a44] 
21:05:06 [4a44] ** Explanation of the output **
21:05:06 [4a44] <1–2 short sentences explaining how the story and questions match the selected topic(s) and the learning goals: general speech-language therapy goals>
21:05:06 [4a44] 
21:05:06 [4a44] STRICT RULES:
21:05:06 [4a44] - Do NOT include any preamble, commentary, or meta-text (no "Here is your story", "Sure!", etc.).
21:05:06 [4a44] - The story text (between Title and End) MUST be between 70 and 100 words.
21:05:06 [4a44] - Generate 5–7 WH-questions using ONLY WHO / WHAT / WHERE (no HOW / WHY at this age tier).
21:05:06 [4a44] - Every question's answer MUST appear word-for-word in the story text. Do NOT ask about anything not stated.
21:05:06 [4a44] - Refer to characters by name (not pronouns) in the story so questions like "WHO is in the story?" are answerable.
21:05:06 [4a44] - Do NOT add sections beyond Title, story text, End, Questions, and Explanation.
